WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.448 --> 00:00:13.369
The air is still. There is a creaking sound.

2
00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:19.036
The lid of the ancient coffin slowly opens. Everyone holds their breath.

3
00:00:19.037 --> 00:00:25.753
I gasp, then breathe a sigh of reliefㅡI see nothing but darkness inside the coffin.

4
00:00:25.754 --> 00:00:32.109
But the coffin is not empty. Someoneㅡor somethingㅡis in the coffin.

5
00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:42.500
It is a mummy. It rises with a jerky movement, and I gasp again. Its face and its entire body are covered loosely in bandages.

6
00:00:42.501 --> 00:00:50.333
The ragged bandages are yellow with age. We are frightened, but our eyes are fixed on this incredible sight.

7
00:00:50.334 --> 00:00:54.419
The mummy sits up completely and slowly turns to us.

8
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:58.657
It begins to mutter ancient prayers that echo through the chamber.

9
00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:02.410
"What's it saying?" I ask a fellow archeologist.

10
00:01:02.411 --> 00:01:10.983
"We must stay with him," my colleague answers, in a strange monotone. "We must stay with him . . . forever."

11
00:01:10.984 --> 00:01:13.062
Did that scene sound familiar?

12
00:01:13.063 --> 00:01:24.163
It could have come from any number of horror movies: The Mummy, The Mummy's Revenge, The Mummy's Ghost, The Mummy's Hand, or The Mummy's Tomb.

13
00:01:24.164 --> 00:01:30.645
The mummy is a fixture in Hollywood movies, but what is the real story of the mummy?

14
00:01:30.646 --> 00:01:37.558
Where do mummies come from? How are they made? Is there really a mummy's curse?

15
00:01:37.559 --> 00:01:40.368
A mummy is a preserved corpse.

16
00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:49.201
Most mummies were made deliberately, but some mummies have been preserved accidentally due to environmental conditions.

17
00:01:49.202 --> 00:01:53.534
The earliest mummy dates back to 3300 BC.

18
00:01:53.535 --> 00:01:58.604
It is a man with red hair and is displayed in the British Museum.

19
00:01:58.605 --> 00:02:02.278
He was buried in the hot desert sand in Africa.

20
00:02:02.279 --> 00:02:08.358
The hot, dry weather dried the body and prevented the flesh from decaying.

21
00:02:08.359 --> 00:02:18.337
By 3030 BC, it was common practice in ancient Egypt to mummify the dead bodies of kings, queens, and the nobility.

22
00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:25.098
This practice continued for over three thousand years and produced over seventy million mummies.

23
00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:30.094
Ancient Egypt is not the only civilization to preserve its dead.

24
00:02:30.095 --> 00:02:35.316
Ancient cultures in Peru and Siberia also practiced mummification.

25
00:02:35.317 --> 00:02:41.494
People in these cultures preserved the bodies of the dead because they believed in life after death.

26
00:02:41.495 --> 00:02:47.161
They believed that a person's body must retain its physical appearance after death.

27
00:02:47.162 --> 00:02:51.986
The best way to do this was to preserve the body by mummification.

28
00:02:51.987 --> 00:03:03.141
In ancient Egypt, the mummification process took seventy days and was done by special priests who performed prayers at different stages in the process.

29
00:03:03.142 --> 00:03:07.714
These priests needed a detailed knowledge of human anatomy.

30
00:03:07.715 --> 00:03:14.135
The first step in mummification was removing all parts of the body that would decay quickly.

31
00:03:14.136 --> 00:03:20.377
First, a hook was stuck up the deceased person's nose and the brain was pulled out.

32
00:03:20.378 --> 00:03:25.703
This was a delicate procedure because the face could easily be disfigured.

33
00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:33.614
The priest then removed the internal organs, leaving only the heart, which was believed to be the center of intelligence.

34
00:03:33.615 --> 00:03:36.122
The organs were preserved separately.

35
00:03:36.123 --> 00:03:46.933
The stomach, liver, lungs, and intestines were placed in special jars called canopic jars, which were later buried with the mummy.

36
00:03:46.934 --> 00:03:53.508
Next, the priests used a kind of salt called natron to dry out the body.

37
00:03:53.509 --> 00:04:00.016
All moisture was removed and the result was a very dried-out but recognizable human form.

38
00:04:00.017 --> 00:04:07.259
The next part in the process was to fill the sunken areas of the body with linen and add false eyes.

39
00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:15.663
Then, the body was covered in oil, spices, and perfume and wrapped in linen bandages coated with resin.

40
00:04:15.664 --> 00:04:18.830
Each mummy needed hundreds of yards of linen.

41
00:04:18.831 --> 00:04:30.724
The priests carefully wound long strips of linen around the body, sometimes even wrapping each finger and toe separately before wrapping the entire hand or foot.

42
00:04:30.725 --> 00:04:37.315
Charms were placed among the linen strips, and prayers were written on some strips for protection.

43
00:04:37.316 --> 00:04:43.979
At several stages, the body was coated with warm resin, and the wrapping resumed once again.

44
00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:50.968
When the wrapping was finished, the priests covered the body with a shroud. The mummy was complete.

45
00:04:50.969 --> 00:04:56.368
People have been interested in mummies for thousands of years, although their reasons have varied.

46
00:04:56.369 --> 00:05:06.296
Soon after the pharaohs were mummified and buried, thieves became very interested in their mummies because they were buried in tombs filled with golden treasures.

47
00:05:06.297 --> 00:05:15.122
Tomb robbing became so common that the mummies were moved to secret burial places, where some remained undisturbed for thousands of years.

48
00:05:15.123 --> 00:05:26.094
In the Middle Ages, it was believed that certain body parts of the mummies had medicinal properties, and so they were ground up into "mummy powder" for use in potions.

49
00:05:26.095 --> 00:05:34.429
In 1798, Napoleon invaded Egypt and took scientists with him to record Egyptian culture and religion.

50
00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:41.928
The research of these scientists was later published in Europe and included their investigation of Egyptian mummies.

51
00:05:41.929 --> 00:05:48.682
"Egyptology," or the study of ancient Egypt, became very popular among the upper classes.

52
00:05:48.683 --> 00:05:57.008
European aristocrats became fond of collecting mummies; they would even hold special parties at which a mummy would be unwrapped.

53
00:05:57.009 --> 00:06:01.743
Many nobles funded or led archeological expeditions to Egypt.

54
00:06:01.744 --> 00:06:07.575
However, most of the ancient tombs they explored had already been disturbed or robbed.

55
00:06:07.576 --> 00:06:16.398
In 1891, a young Englishman named Howard Carter arrived in Egypt and trained for many years as an archeologist.

56
00:06:16.399 --> 00:06:21.049
Carter was convinced that there was at least one undiscovered tomb.

57
00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:28.063
Under the patronage of Lord Carnarvon, a wealthy English aristocrat, Carter searched for tombs.

58
00:06:28.064 --> 00:06:41.126
In 1922, Lord Carnarvon summoned him to England to tell him the funding for his research had run out, but Carter convinced Lord Carnarvon to fund one final expedition.

59
00:06:41.127 --> 00:06:48.292
On November 4, 1922, Carter's team discovered an ancient doorway that was still sealed.

60
00:06:48.293 --> 00:06:51.368
On the doorway was the name "Tutankhamun".

61
00:06:51.369 --> 00:06:57.946
Upon discovering the untouched tomb, Howard Carter told Lord Carnarvon to come to Egypt immediately.

62
00:06:57.947 --> 00:07:02.022
When the tomb door was opened, there were many celebrations.

63
00:07:02.023 --> 00:07:12.356
They found the tomb undisturbed, and inside were many treasures, including the mummy of Pharaoh Tutankhamun and three gold coffins nested inside each other.

64
00:07:12.357 --> 00:07:19.016
There were rumors that Carter also found a tablet inscribed with a curse but immediately hid it from his workers.

65
00:07:19.017 --> 00:07:26.753
A number of mysterious deaths followed, and it was thought that Tutankhamun's mummy had cursed those who had disturbed his tomb.

66
00:07:26.754 --> 00:07:30.932
First, Lord Carnarvon died in Cairo of pneumonia.

67
00:07:30.933 --> 00:07:33.083
This created a media frenzy.

68
00:07:33.084 --> 00:07:36.672
News of the mummy's curse circulated around the world.

69
00:07:36.673 --> 00:07:50.743
Legend has it, that by 1929, eleven of the people connected with the discovery of the tomb had died, including two of Lord Carnarvon's relatives, and Carter's personal secretary, Richard Bethell.

70
00:07:50.744 --> 00:08:00.808
The bodies of the dead pharaohs, queens, and nobility of ancient Egypt weren't the only things to be mummifiedㅡthe ancient Egyptians also mummified animals!

71
00:08:00.809 --> 00:08:06.634
Some cults in ancient Egypt worshipped animals, like the cat goddess Bast.

72
00:08:06.635 --> 00:08:13.478
Thousands of cats were mummified for sale to pilgrims, who presented them as offerings to the cat goddess.

73
00:08:13.479 --> 00:08:18.227
Vast cemeteries have been discovered containing thousands of buried cats.

74
00:08:18.228 --> 00:08:23.789
It is likely that cats were bred especially to be sacrificed to the Egyptian cat goddess.

75
00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:36.372
The Egyptians believed that their gods could become certain animalsㅡcats, hawks, crocodiles, and bullsㅡand these animals were protected, worshipped, and mummified at death.

76
00:08:36.373 --> 00:08:43.379
Much of our knowledge of Egyptian mummification comes from one historian in particular: Herodotus.

77
00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:52.547
Herodotus was an ancient Greek who observed and recorded how bodies were mummified on his travels through Egypt in 450 BC.

78
00:08:52.548 --> 00:08:56.289
Mummification was practiced for over three thousand years.

79
00:08:56.290 --> 00:09:00.707
In 30 BC, Egypt became a province of the Roman Empire.

80
00:09:00.708 --> 00:09:10.861
The Roman presence in ancient Egypt eventually led to the decline of the practice of mummification and the replacement of ancient religions with Christianity.

81
00:09:10.862 --> 00:09:17.772
Nowadays, the technology of x-rays and computers allows us to examine mummies without unwrapping them.

82
00:09:17.773 --> 00:09:20.272
Their bones can be scanned and studied.

83
00:09:20.273 --> 00:09:23.663
DNA samples can be extracted and analyzed.

84
00:09:23.664 --> 00:09:28.426
Although dead for thousands of years, the mummies continue to speak to us.
